<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="1988667a">&lt;p&gt;
    Test data is typically a combination of generated and production data. For certain test purposes, using production
    data may be essential. In other cases, generated or synthetic data may be just as effective.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Reasons for harvesting production data for testing purposes include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Reduces risk of unknown data anomalies, which limit post-development changes to satisfy any irregularities based on
        the test data selection
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Enforces application security to adhere to any data security policies
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Permits end-to-end processing, mimicking what happens in production
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Increases confidence in test results for the purpose of certifying application production readiness
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    When using production data for testing purposes, careful measures should be taken to avoid any test transactions
    triggering events in production.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Reasons for generating synthetic data for testing purposes include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Production data contains sensitive user information that violates certain regulations (e.g., HIPAA)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Accessible test management tools or data generation tools that have the ability to specify the type of data fields
        and values needed as well as the ability to generate test data
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In situations where synthetic test data is manually generated, allocate the proper amount of time as
    this exercise is often labor intensive and time consuming. 
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
